  "metric_definitions": {
    "wape": "sum(|actual - pred|) / sum(|actual|) * 100% (volume-weighted percentage error)",
    "rmse": "sqrt(mean((actual - pred)^2)) in currency units (INR)",
    "mae": "mean(|actual - pred|) in currency units (INR)",
    "r2": "1 - SS_res / SS_tot (coefficient of determination)",
    "bias": "sum(pred - actual) / sum(|actual|) * 100% (positive = overforecasting, negative = underforecasting)",
    "mase": "mae_model / mean(|diff(y_train)|) (relative to training naive random walk)"
  },
  "investigation_wape_vs_negative_r2": {
    "finding": "Mathematical Divergence between WAPE and R2: 1. Profit scale level: Actual net profit values for enterprise supermarkets average ~Rs. 100,000 to 450,000. A model error of Rs. 30,000 to 50,000 yields a modest WAPE of ~15-20% because WAPE scales relative to total profit volume sum(|y|). 2. Restricted Variance in Validation Window: Over a 6-month validation horizon across 2 companies (N=12), the natural variance of net profit around its sample mean SS_tot is small. When SS_res (sum of squared errors) exceeds this narrow SS_tot, R2 = 1 - SS_res/SS_tot becomes negative. 3. Non-reliance on MAPE: As required, MAPE is explicitly disregarded for profit forecasting because actual monthly net profit can approach zero or turn negative, triggering division by near-zero and yielding distorted astronomical percentages. WAPE and MAE are the authoritative, mathematically sound criteria for model selection."
  },
